# Zig lineage 1 — optimization submission

The final service retains two of three preregistered hypotheses: opportunistic
terminal group commit and omission of the unused retry digest on evaluate.
Bounded transport-buffer reuse was measured separately and dropped as an
operational tie. There is no rewrite, kernel replacement, admission batching,
intentional group wait, or storage-setting change.

## Build and validation

Use the pinned Linux AArch64 broker for every build and test:

```sh
python3 run.py './build.sh' 120
python3 run.py './tests/run.sh' 180
python3 run.py './service DATADIR SOCKET_PATH'
python3 run.py 'ZIG_OPTIMIZE=Debug SERVICE_OUTPUT=tmp/service-debug ./build.sh' 120
python3 run.py 'ZIG_OPTIMIZE=Debug SERVICE_BINARY=tmp/service-debug ./tests/run.sh' 180
```

Default build is Zig 0.16.0 ReleaseSafe, dynamically linked to the designated
/adapter/librosetta.so and RocksDB 7.8.3-2, with adapter rpath. build.sh accepts
SERVICE_OUTPUT and now SERVICE_SOURCE for isolated experiment reproduction;
default source remains src/main.zig and default output is service. DATADIR's
parent must exist. SOCKET_PATH must be fresh. Exclusive RocksDB ownership and
zero exit on a successful draining shutdown are preserved.

Final ReleaseSafe and Debug each passed all three scheduler unit tests, public
smoke and 29 service tests. One additional discovered test explicitly skips:
the original v1 executable is absent from this workspace. A new test runs the
actual supplied v2 predecessor, kills it after its first terminal write, then
checks optimized recovery preserves receipt bytes, ID indexes and cancellation
intent. The predecessor is retained at tmp/baseline/service with its sources,
tests, build script, HANDOFF.md and EXPERIMENTS.md. Do not confuse it with v1.

Build/test logs are tmp/combined-{build,tests}.log and
tmp/combined-debug-{build,tests}.log. The suite builds tests/write_observer.c as
a test-only LD_PRELOAD DSO. No observer or test hooks are linked into service.
External holds replace inherited tests' assumptions that pending work remains
slow enough to query or cancel. Test-client startup retries only transient
ECONNREFUSED between bind and listen. Initial failed attempts are retained and
explained in EXPERIMENTS.md.

## Implementation and invariant preservation

- src/main.zig collects up to 32 already-completed jobs beginning at checkpoint+1.
  It stops at the first unfinished job or the bound. It does not wait to fill a
  group. All receipt puts, pending/cancel deletions, checkpoint advance and final
  accounting values are one synchronous WAL WriteBatch. Capacity and ownership
  are released only after the write succeeds; notifications follow durability.
- The event loop still owns every write and client operation. It cannot process
  a cancellation between building a terminal group and entering rocksdb_write.
  That C call_entry is the commitment boundary for every member. A previously
  acknowledged cancellation is reflected in the receipt. Queued/running work
  retains the predecessor's ownership and cancellation rules.
- Evaluate retains validation, decoding and native v2 execution but skips the
  SHA-256 digest used only by durable admission/recovery. Its internal unused
  digest is initialized to zero. Submit and recovery compute the exact existing
  digest; priority remains excluded and immutable on retries.
- src/storage.zig retains all open/cache/buffer/compression/WAL/sync settings.
  It adds counts of C write attempts and successful synchronous C writes.
  src/dispatch.zig is unchanged: four workers, HHHN while both queues have work,
  FIFO within priority, no cycle advance for a sole queue, and admission-ordered
  terminal commitment. Input arenas remain owned until native return.
- Schema 1, v1 missing-priority fallback, v2 priorities, corruption refusal,
  unlimited receipts for the bounded trial, ID indexes, draining behavior,
  decimal/hex wire encodings and all resource limits remain unchanged.

Successful draining shutdown emits one JSON diagnostic line to stderr:
write_calls, sync_write_successes, terminal_batch_histogram (index=jobs per
terminal write; index 0 unused), and intentional_group_wait_ns=0. These counters
are process-local and reset on restart. sync_write_successes counts successful
sync=true C writes; it is not a physical syscall count. Crashed processes may
have no final diagnostic, so it is not a recovery oracle.

## Correctness and durability evidence

Independent stopped-writer inspection checks every disk invariant. External
write-barrier tests show a crash before a 32-job transition retains the complete
old state, a crash after it retains the complete new state, and an injected
terminal-write failure preserves all pending members. Sixty-five cancelled jobs
commit in groups 32/32/1. Tests check accepted cancellation before the C boundary,
one durable intent for repeated cancellation, and too_late for cancellation
overlapping an already-entered terminal write after its barrier is released.
Large evaluate results match the direct supplied v2 oracle, and the durable
version stores the independently computed digest.

ReleaseSafe/Debug and these process-crash tests do not establish native ASan,
whole-service race freedom, power-loss safety, or exhaustive interleavings.
Exact external native-entry fairness qualification was not repeated; unchanged
production queue selection has its original three unit cases. No evaluator,
other workspace, external source or Bitcoin library was accessed.

## Performance and limitations

Local measurements use one separate warmup plus seven fresh datadirs per case
and variant, cyclic variant order, serial execution and no observer during
timing. Requests and directories are prepared outside timing. Eight clients
have window 32 each. Startup/shutdown and independent inspection are excluded;
client/socket/JSON costs are included. All trials and warmups are retained.
The supplied filesystem does not provide independently provisioned fresh block
volumes, and the campaign-frozen workload generators were not supplied. These
measurements are local evidence, not campaign qualification or a language ranking.

Final short comparison medians (baseline -> optimized):

| Local case | Seconds | Interpretation |
|---|---|---|
| 4,096 tiny evaluates | .06204 -> .06167 | Operational tie |
| 64 padded 256 KiB prefix evaluates | .13320 -> .09702 | 27.2% lower median |
| 2,048 durable jobs | 2.42270 -> 1.35369 | 44.1% lower median, wide ranges |
| 2,048 mixed-priority jobs | 1.07838 -> 1.61496 | 49.8% higher median, wide ranges |

Mixed median per-run p99 receipt latency was 423.15/422.81 ms normal/high for
baseline and 368.00/371.28 ms optimized. This adverse mixed runtime result is
retained alongside the earlier isolated H1 mixed improvement. It prevents a
uniform speedup claim. Full ranges, raw repetitions, and the separately declared
longer mixed follow-up are recorded in EXPERIMENTS.md and tmp/*/summary.json.

The separately preregistered 16,384-job mixed follow-up completed all seven
measured pairs. Median elapsed was 8.99713 s [5.59996, 13.97949] baseline versus
5.64341 s [3.48723, 6.84049] optimized, 37.3% lower; the optimized run was faster
in every measured pair. Median per-run normal/high p99 was 495.71/490.58 ms versus
391.21/391.15 ms. This supports retaining group commit with its observed write
reduction, while preserving the contrary short-run result and storage-variance
limitation. Results are separate, not pooled; no universal speedup is claimed.

A separate instrumented 2,048-job mixed run observed 4,097 baseline WAL syncs
versus 2,542 optimized, matching C-write counts and RocksDB's own cumulative WAL
statistics. Admissions stayed at 2,048. Terminal writes fell from 2,048 to 493,
with group sizes 1..9. All-file libc sync successes were 4,108 versus 2,553.
RocksDB reported zero flush/compaction bytes and zero stalls in these small
instrumented runs; they do not exercise sustained compaction pressure. Timing
from instrumented runs is excluded from performance comparisons. Full statistics
and traces are under tmp/write-observations/.

Reproduce local measurements into a new output path:

```sh
python3 run.py 'python3 tests/benchmark.py --output tmp/new-comparison --variants baseline:tmp/baseline/service optimized:service' 300
python3 run.py 'python3 tests/benchmark.py --output tmp/new-long-mixed --variants baseline:tmp/baseline/service optimized:service --cases mixed --count-scale 8' 300
```

The user identified the supplied v2 predecessor as qualified. Gate artifacts
were not supplied or inspected. Existing wire interpretations remain: unknown
fields are reserved; known-ID retries preserve existing/conflict during drain;
evaluate is normal-priority and rejects a priority field; invalid correlation
uses an empty string; shutdown permits one second for final output flushing;
terminal notifications are best effort and receipts are authoritative.

